## Runtime workflow seats - edited with the whole instruction set in view

A runtime seat is an agent a program spawns (a workflow file, an orchestrator script, a skill naming a
`subagent_type`), not one a human routes to. Its effective prompt is the union of four sources:

| # | Source | Where it lives |
|---|--------|----------------|
| 1 | the agent body | `.claude/agents/<name>.md` |
| 2 | the call-site prompt | the text the caller passes when it spawns the seat |
| 3 | interpolated constants | model-policy tables, preamble/relay blocks, schema fragments the caller splices in |
| 4 | every other call site | the same seat spawned from a second place, with different text |

<!-- BLOCK: | Seat | Agent file | Call sites (file:line) | Constant blocks it receives | -->
{RUNTIME_SEAT_ROSTER}

Read all four together before touching a seat's file - editing one blind to the others is how contradictions enter:

| Rule | Detail |
|------|--------|
| Facts first | judge on correctness, consistency and contradiction - never on prose taste |
| Contradiction | quote both texts and report it; reconcile silently only when one side names something that no longer exists - then fix that side and say which |
| Contract lines | never compress a line a call site depends on: a return-schema field, a tool name, a command, an id, a threshold, a status string |
| One writer | a seat's file belongs to exactly one batch; its checker re-reads every call site above |

## Delegation (binds every agent this skill spawns)

One subagent = one observable bounded deliverable (one batch), ~<=5 files, ~<=10 steps; larger units split into N
tasks, all spawned in one message. A batch over ~10 files splits into spawns of <=5 files, disjointness preserved.

Every spawn brief carries six labeled fields, since a subagent starts with an empty context: GOAL (task + why), ROLE (owns, must not touch), SCOPE (paths/commands in and out), CONTEXT (done, by whom, in parallel), CONSUMER (who uses the result + its shape), DONE (acceptance + return: verdict first, evidence, bounded; long output to a file, path returned).
A bare one-line task is never enough.

## Phase 1 - GATHER (parallel, read-only)

Spawn read-only `Explore` agents in one message:

**(a) change-fact list.** For git scopes, agents read the scope diff and return a terse fact list: added / renamed
/ removed modules, entry points, env-var names, commands, dependencies, tables, lint or CI gates, behaviours.
`session` -> no agent: the coordinator writes the list from its own conversation context. `all` -> skipped:
Phase 2 verifies every fact regardless of any diff.

**(b) target inventory.** Resolve the concrete file list per batch by enumeration, never from a hardcoded list:

<!-- BLOCK: ONE fenced bash block re-deriving the per-batch file list (ls / find -name '*.md' | sort / git ls-files), ENDING with a `sort | uniq -d` disjointness assertion over the concatenated batch lists -->
{ENUMERATION_BASH}

Whatever that block contains, these bind the enumeration:
- Prune `.claude/reports/**` (run-artifact snapshots, often full `CLAUDE.md` copies) and `.claude/worktrees/**`
  (other checkouts of this repo) from every `find`: never memory, whatever the EXCLUDED table says.
- Re-resolve the runtime-seat roster's call sites: grep each caller file for the seat name; roster line numbers rot,
  today's bytes decide.
- Enumerate the per-agent memory stores over the whole tree (`find` / `ls`), never a roster: a store is born wherever
  a session ran, and one whose agent is gone is still swept. Classify each LIVE / ORPHAN / UNDECIDED per
  `references/memory-guide.md` rule 5, with the evidence beside the verdict (the EXCLUDED runtime/archived path
  containing it, or the session root it is). Record each store's index separately from its rows: it is rewritten last.
- `{REACH}` = `REFS` only: per ALWAYS-LOADED instruction file, list the paths it names and tells its reader to open,
  keeping the git-tracked ones outside the instruction tree. Each merges into the `{FILE_LIST}` of the batch owning its
  citing file, with its own `wc -l` baseline, so the disjointness assertion runs over the merged lists. Cited from two
  batches -> it joins only the batch of the first citing file in path-sort order; the other batch reports findings on
  it, never edits it. At `MEMORY` no outside file enters any `{FILE_LIST}`.

Filter the result through the EXCLUDED table, one narrow exception: discover and classify per-agent stores before
applying runtime/archived parent exclusions, then keep only those stores' index + rows for the rule-5 sweep/salvage;
parent, sibling and any other file stay excluded. An explicit exclusion of memory or the store, a user-narrowed scope and
every non-negotiable exclusion still win - never read, sweep or salvage an expressly excluded store.
Record `wc -l` per file (the non-growth baseline) and flag broken references - a memory file pointing at a path that
no longer exists, including refs into docs (resolution only). Assert disjointness: the concatenated per-batch lists
through `sort | uniq -d` MUST be empty; a duplicate is a defect - fix the batch table before Phase 2.

Phase 1 output: the change-fact list, per-batch `path:lines` lists, broken refs, re-resolved seat call sites, each
per-agent store as `index + rows` with its LIVE/ORPHAN/UNDECIDED verdict and evidence, and at `REFS` the outside
references already merged into those lists.

## Phase 2 - SYNC (one bounded agent per batch, all in one message)

Spawn one subagent per non-empty batch, all in a single message, each with its file list, the change-fact list and
the prompt below verbatim, every placeholder substituted. Owners come from the roster table; resolve each against
recursive `find .claude/agents -type f -name '*.md'` (actual `name:`) before spawning; no project owner, or a name
that no longer resolves -> built-in `general-purpose`.

```
## memory-sync batch: {BATCH} (scope {SCOPE}, depth {DEPTH})

GOAL: this project's instruction memory has drifted from the code and bloated. Make it TRUE again and SMALLER.
ROLE: you own exactly the files listed below. You touch nothing else - no source, no docs, no secrets, no task
      state, no hook file, no other batch's memory file. {M} sibling agents sync other batches in parallel.
SCOPE - files you may EDIT (nothing else), as path:baseline-lines: {FILE_LIST}
READ EVIDENCE: minimum actual code, call sites and non-secret configuration key names needed to verify this batch;
      read-only outside EDIT scope. Never open secrets; docs remain resolution-only. Explicit user read exclusions bind.
REACH: {REACH}. At MEMORY the editable set is the instruction surface only - a tracked reference OUTSIDE it is
      RESOLUTION-CHECKED only, and every compression or fact finding on it is REPORTED, never applied. At REFS each
      cited outside reference IS in the SCOPE list above (merged into the batch owning its citing file) and is
      editable too, one report row each; non-growth still binds.
Change facts from scope: {FACTS}   (empty -> verify EVERYTHING against current code)
Broken refs already found in your files: {BROKEN_REFS}
Today, for `last_updated` (coordinator resolves it with `date +%F`, never hardcoded): {DATE}
CONTEXT (already resolved - do NOT re-derive): the file list, its per-file line baselines and the broken refs,
      all given in this prompt.
READ FIRST (paths, not pasted prose):
  .claude/skills/memory-sync/references/memory-guide.md   - layer decision tree, compression patterns, obvious gate
  .claude/skills/memory-sync/references/agent-audit.md    - agents and skills batches ONLY: the standing re-audit
  .claude/skills/memory-sync/references/prompting-guide.md - the merged rule table + lossless guard, step 3 below
  .claude/skills/memory-sync/SKILL.md                     - the checkable-fact catalogue (verification commands), the house invariants

Per file, in THIS priority order:
1. PRIMARY - fact synchronization:
   - VERIFY every checkable claim against the repo, by running the command in the fact catalogue (grep/read the
     ACTUAL code - never from memory, never from another doc).
   - ADD the facts this scope introduced that belong in THIS file (add gate + non-growth budget still bind).
   - REMOVE every fact that is no longer true: deleted module, renamed path, retired gate, dead command.
2. SIDE EFFECTS - never at the expense of facts:
   - DEDUPLICATE: a fact repeated across files keeps ONE canonical home; copies become a pointer
     ("canonical: <file> section"). Canonical home in ANOTHER batch -> REPORT it, do not edit a foreign file.
   - COMPRESS verbose passages into this repo's dense table/pointer style; meaning intact, lines down.
3. PROMPT QUALITY - instruction files only (CLAUDE.md/rules/AGENTS.md/agent/skill bodies, never code/docs):
   - Apply `references/prompting-guide.md`'s rule table; rewrite ONLY where its lossless guard allows it -
     never touch a path, version, flag, threshold, model id, incident-backed `!=`/NEVER row or canonical list.
   - `{DEPTH}` = NORMAL: fix a violation ONLY where it coincides with a fact/dedup edit already touching that
     line; otherwise REPORT it. `{DEPTH}` = HARD: rewrite every remaining violation.
4. MEMORY BATCHES ONLY (the memory dir, a per-agent store) - sweep memory ITSELF under the five rules in
   .claude/skills/memory-sync/references/memory-guide.md; they are not restated here. A DELETE on the
   DUPLICATION ground REQUIRES the quoted context line already carrying that fact, returned below - an unproven
   duplicate STAYS. Rewrite a store's INDEX last, over the surviving set only. Your store's rule-5 verdict comes
   WITH your file list: LIVE or UNDECIDED -> sweep it and report it, never remove. Proven ORPHAN -> SALVAGE THEN REMOVE,
   only when both stores are authorized members of this SAME owned batch and neither is expressly excluded:
   judge every row by rules 1-4, MOVE each survivor into the SAME agent's LIVE store merged with any row on that
   subject, rewrite THAT store's index, then remove the store DIRECTORY only, never its parent, a sibling, or the
   workspace/report/archive containing it. A salvaged row is a MOVE, not an ADD: the receiving store may exceed its
   own baseline by at most the lines the removed store gave up, and the TOTAL delta still binds. Return the
   per-file `memory` object below; a non-memory batch omits it.

NON-GROWTH (hard): every file ends <= the baseline given beside its path in SCOPE; delete before you add. TWO authorized growths, nothing else: the PASS A frontmatter `paths:` repair at HARD depth on a MISSING or TOO_NARROW verdict, and a rule-5 SALVAGE, bounded by the lines the removed store gave up.

HARD constraints:
- SELF-SYNC CARVE-OUT: if this skill's own SKILL.md or references are in your batch, facts may be CORRECTED but
  SCOPE DECISIONS may NOT - the in-scope surface list, the EXCLUDED table, the docs exclusion, the non-growth
  directive.
- Bump `last_updated:` to {DATE} in every file you edit - from `date +%F`, NEVER a hardcoded date.
- Preserve `[DICT: ...]` headers wherever they exist.
- Stable NUMBERED rule ids are NEVER renumbered and NEVER reordered - other files cite them POSITIONALLY, so a
  reorder silently repoints every citation. New rule -> APPEND at the end.
- Keep frontmatter valid; repair `paths:` globs that no longer match anything real.
- Language policy: {LANGUAGE_POLICY}. An intentional trigger alias stripped as a "violation" is a REGRESSION.
- NEVER write a secret VALUE (names, paths and ids are fine). Never open a secrets file.
- Read-only git only: diff/log/show/ls-files/status/merge-base/symbolic-ref. Never checkout/reset/restore/revert.
- Never run a build, a test suite or a migration as a side effect.
- VERIFY-ONLY members of your batch: check that refs RESOLVE; never edit their content.
- A block delimited by another tool's begin/end markers (e.g. `<!-- BEGIN brewcode:semble -->` ..
  `<!-- END brewcode:semble -->`) is owned by that setup, which re-creates it on upgrade: never compress, move,
  dedup or delete it, both marker lines included. Dedup from the other copy; report it as `managed by <setup>`.
- RUNTIME SEATS: a file of yours that the runtime-seat roster names is edited only after you have read all four
  instruction sources it lists. Quote and REPORT contradictions; never compress a contract line. Return them
  under "runtime_seats".
- Uncertain whether a fact is stale? Verify in code. STILL uncertain -> LEAVE IT and REPORT it. Never guess.

CONSUMER: an independent read-only checker re-verifies every claim you make, then the coordinator's report table.
DONE - return JSON, one entry per file, empty lists if clean:
{"<path>": {"added": [...], "removed": [...], "fixed": [...], "dedup": [...], "compressed": N,
            "lines_before": N, "lines_after": N, "broken_refs": [...], "uncertain": [...],
            "memory": {"verdict": "kept|merged|promoted|deleted", "event": "promoted|deleted|none",
                       "duplicate_quote": "verbatim context line proving the duplicate - REQUIRED to delete on that ground",
                       "store": {"verdict": "live|orphan|undecided", "evidence": "the EXCLUDED runtime/archived path containing it, or empty-on-sight, or the session root it is",
                                 "removed": "<store dir removed | none>",
                                 "salvaged": [{"row": "...", "into": "<live store path>", "merged_with": "<row | none>"}]}},
            "prompt_quality": [{"rule": N, "line": N, "before": "...", "after": "..."}],
            "runtime_seats": [{"seat": "...", "sources_read": ["..."], "contradictions": [{"a": "...", "b": "...", "where": "..."}]}]}}
At {DEPTH} = HARD each entry ALSO carries a nested "hard": {...} object, shaped exactly as in references/hard-sync.md.
```

At `{DEPTH}` = `HARD`, the batch prompt also carries, verbatim:

```
DEPTH = HARD. Also run the two passes in .claude/skills/memory-sync/references/hard-sync.md, in this order:
  1. `paths:` PRECISION audit  - every rule's glob must match reality and cover exactly its subject.
  2. OBVIOUS-KNOWLEDGE PURGE   - delete what any competent model already knows; keep only domain facts.
These passes only shrink, except PASS A frontmatter `paths:` repair on MISSING/TOO_NARROW. The separate common
rule-5 salvage exception still requires the same-agent, same-owned-batch, authorized-store gates and total budget.
```

Cross-batch dedup (canonical home in another batch's file) is reported, never edited by the batch owner; the
coordinator applies the cross-batch pointer after both batches return.

## Phase 3 - VERIFY (independent checkers - this phase GATES the report)

After every batch returns, spawn read-only `Explore` checkers in one message - **one per edited batch, NEVER
the agent that wrote it**. Each gets that batch's returned JSON, its edited file list, and this prompt verbatim -
every placeholder substituted:

```
## memory-sync VERIFY: batch {BATCH} (scope {SCOPE}, depth {DEPTH}, reach {REACH})

GOAL: a batch agent claims it made instruction memory truer and smaller. Prove or disprove every claim against
      the CODE - never against the doc that agent just wrote. This phase GATES the report.
ROLE: independent read-only checker. You did NOT write these files and you EDIT nothing - you return verdicts.
VERIFY TARGETS: {FILE_LIST}. READ EVIDENCE: minimum actual code, call sites, non-secret configuration key names
      and cited instruction context needed to verify these targets; read-only, not extra edit targets. Never open
      secrets; docs remain resolution-only. Explicit user read exclusions bind; report unavailable evidence.
CONTEXT (already resolved - do NOT re-derive): the batch's returned JSON, the per-file line baselines, this
      repo's git visibility. Sibling checkers verify the other batches in parallel.
READ FIRST (paths, not pasted prose):
  .claude/skills/memory-sync/SKILL.md   - the checkable-fact catalogue (verification commands), the house invariants
